# GitHub Workflow (compulsory)

All work follows this sequence. Scope: all work.

## 1. Issue first

Before creating a new issue, **search for an existing one** using `gh issue list --repo <repo> --label <scope> --state open` and keyword searches. Reuse an existing issue if one covers the same work. Only create a new issue if no match is found.

Create a GitHub issue using the **Bounded Context Work Item** before any code changes. If the user requests work and no issue exists, create the issue first—never start coding without an issue.

**Body structure (API-created issues):**
```
## Background

[why this is needed]

## Expected outcome

[clear, testable outcome]

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Criterion 1
- [ ] Criterion 2

## Possible solution(s)

1. Option A - ...
2. Option B - ...

## References

- Link to doc
- Related issue #N
```
Title format `type(scope): description` provides type and scope for labels.

- Title format: `type(scope): description` (e.g. `feat(web): add validation`, `chore(tooling): add commitlint`)
- Labels `type` and `scope` are auto-applied from the title
- Assignee: creator is auto-assigned (commissioning developer)

## 2. Branch

Checkout/create branch from `main` using `<type>/123-slug` (issue number + short slug). Accepted prefixes in this repo include `feat`, `fix`, `chore`, and `docs`.

## 3. Plan

When an agent creates an execution plan (e.g. todo list), post it as a comment on the issue before starting work.

## 4. Work

Make changes within the bounded context of the issue. When contracts change: run `pnpm turbo run generate --filter=@forge/graphql` in the same PR and tick "Regeneration Required: yes" in PR template.

## 5. Commits

Produce a series of commits—one per small block of work. Each commit must use conventional format (`feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`, `docs:` etc.). Atomic and reviewable.

**Before committing**, verify the current branch's PR is not already merged:
```
gh pr list --repo JesusFilm/forge --head "$(git branch --show-current)" --state merged --json number
```
If the result is non-empty, **stop** — do not commit or push. Create a new branch from `main` instead.

## 6. PR

Open PR targeting `main`. Use same title format as issue: `type(scope): description`. Fill PR template (Summary, Contracts Changed, Regeneration Required, Validation). Include `Resolves #123` in description.

**Creating/updating PRs and issues with multiline bodies:** Never pass `\n` escape sequences in MCP tool string parameters — they render as literal text. Always use `gh` CLI with a HEREDOC:
```
gh pr create --title "type(scope): description" --body "$(cat <<'EOF'
## Summary
...
EOF
)"
```
Same applies to `gh issue create`, `gh pr edit`, and `gh issue edit`.

## Resolving merge conflicts

**Never rebase a feature branch to resolve PR merge conflicts.** Use `git merge` instead:
```
git fetch upstream main && git merge upstream/main --no-edit
```
For `pnpm-lock.yaml` conflicts: `git checkout --theirs pnpm-lock.yaml && pnpm install --no-frozen-lockfile`, then stage and commit.

## 7. Checks

Ensure all CI checks pass before marking work complete. Use `gh pr checks <PR> --repo JesusFilm/forge` to verify. Re-run or fix failures.

## 8. Post check passing

Resolve all review comments with follow-up—fix the issue or explain why it doesn't need to be addressed. Add a PR comment summarizing how each comment was handled (fixed or why not addressed).
